<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palanan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palanan is a remote municipality on the Pacific coast of Isabela province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luzon">Luzon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, cut off from the interior by the Sierra Madre mountains. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a>, president of the First Philippine Republic, hid there during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> until he was captured on 23 March 1901 by an American force under Frederick Funston, which had entered disguised as prisoners of Macabebe scouts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palanan.jpg" alt="Palanan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Staff Sgt. Chad L. Simon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buildings_and_roads_in_the_Philippine_town_of_Palanan_are_visible_from_a_P-3C_Orion_aircraft_carrying_U.S._Sailors_Oct._22,_2010_101022-M-ZA787-148.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palanan is one of four remote coastal towns of Isabela that face the Philippine Sea and are separated from the rest of the province by the Sierra Madre. No road links it to the rest of Isabela: it is reached by a short flight from Cauayan to Palanan Airport, by boat from Divilacan or from Dingalan and Baler in Aurora, or by a hike of three to five days over the mountains from San Mariano. Its name is said to come from the Ibanag warning palanammu, a word of caution used because the area was surrounded by Aetas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Unlike other towns of the Cagayan Valley, Palanan was founded on 22 July 1609 by Spanish forces who came by boat from Baler on the Pacific coast. It therefore belonged in turn to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pampanga">Pampanga</a>, Laguna, Tayabas, Nueva Ecija and Nueva Vizcaya before passing to Isabela, and it was served by Franciscan missionaries rather than the Dominicans who worked elsewhere in the valley. Its people were at first Paranan, later joined by local Negritos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> migrants from Baler and outlaws from the Cagayan Valley, and Tagalog rather than Ilocano or Ibanag became the common language.</p><p>From 1900 Palanan served as the last capital of the First Philippine Republic. The capture of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> there on 23 March 1901 by an American force under Frederick Funston, who had gained access to his camp by a ruse, was one of the final chapters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a>.</p><p>In 1978 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-marcos">Ferdinand Marcos</a> proclaimed the area around the town part of the Palanan Wilderness Area, a protected conservation area that President Fidel V. Ramos expanded in 1997 into the Northern Sierra Madre Natural Park.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Palanan is divided into 17 barangays and had 18,091 inhabitants in the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Work began in March 2016 on an 82-kilometre road from Ilagan to Divilacan through the Sierra Madre, following an old logging road, to open the coastal towns to the rest of the province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palanan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aguinaldo was captured at <b>Palanan</b> in March 1901 and swore allegiance to the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a></li></ul></section>
